                  "The owner files a signed application giving its name and principal office, the tenant's residence or place of business or employment, the nature, amount and dates of the claim and facts showing it is a consumer transaction, the certification of no more than five claims that month, and the certification of the demand letter mailed 10 to 180 days before (NY:CCA-1803-A(b)); it pays the filing fee and notice cost. The clerk marks the claim as a consumer transaction and mails notice within five days by first-class and certified mail; the tenant may ask within 14 days for an evening hearing. If the first-class mailing is not returned undeliverable within 30 days, the tenant is presumed to have received notice. If notice cannot be served within four months after filing, the claim is dismissed without prejudice. The owner appears with its witnesses and documents; an owner that does not appear may have its claim dismissed or decided for the tenant. A tenant that does not appear within one hour after the fixed time is in default; notice of the default judgment is mailed to both sides, and the tenant may apply in writing to vacate it under CPLR 5015. A tenant's counterclaim (for example for the deposit) is filed within five days of the notice or later up to the hearing, when the owner may take an adjournment. If all parties appear by attorney the case is transferred to the regular part and the owner pays the added fees or the case is dismissed. The parties may agree in writing to binding arbitration before a court-appointed attorney arbitrator, with no appeal. The rule's requirement of a principal office in New York City yields to CCA 1801-A(a), which requires a principal office in New York State; a statute prevails over an inconsistent court rule.",
